1. What is this?
  this is a reference to the current object whose method or constructor is being executed.
👉 Used to:
    Refer current class instance variables
    Invoke current class methods
    Call current class constructor
    Pass current object as argument
    Return current object

2. Uses of this Keyword (All Cases)
  2.1 Referring to Instance Variables
    class Person {
        private int age;
    
        void setAge(int age) {
            this.age = age; // differentiates instance variable
        }
    }

  2.2 Calling Current Class Method
    class Test {
        void m1() {
            System.out.println("m1");
        }
        void m2() {
            this.m1(); // optional
        }
    }

2.3 Constructor Chaining (this())
    class Employee {
    
        Employee() {
            this(100);
            System.out.println("Default constructor");
        }
    
        Employee(int id) {
            System.out.println("Parameterized constructor: " + id);
        }
    }
  🔑 Rules:
  this() must be first statement
  Avoids code duplication

2.4 Passing this as Method Argument
      class Demo {
          void show(Demo d) {
              System.out.println("Method called");
          }
          void call() {
              show(this);
          }
      }

2.5 Returning this
    class Builder {
        Builder set() {
            return this;
        }
    }
    Used in method chaining / builder pattern.

3. What is super?
    super is a reference to the immediate parent class object.
👉 Used to:
    Access parent class variables
    Call parent class methods
    Call parent class constructor

4. Uses of super Keyword
  4.1 Access Parent Class Variable
    class Parent {
        int x = 10;
    }
    
    class Child extends Parent {
        int x = 20;
    
        void show() {
            System.out.println(super.x); // 10
        }
    }

  4.2 Call Parent Class Method
    class Parent {
        void display() {
            System.out.println("Parent");
        }
    }
    
    class Child extends Parent {
        void display() {
            super.display();
            System.out.println("Child");
        }
    }

  4.3 Call Parent Class Constructor (super())
    class Parent {
        Parent(int x) {
            System.out.println("Parent constructor");
        }
    }
    
    class Child extends Parent {
        Child() {
            super(10);
            System.out.println("Child constructor");
        }
    }
🔑 Rules:
    super() must be first statement
    If not written, compiler adds default super()

5. Constructor Chaining Order (Very Important)
    class A {
        A() { System.out.println("A"); }
    }
    
    class B extends A {
        B() { System.out.println("B"); }
    }
    
    class C extends B {
        C() { System.out.println("C"); }
    }


    Output:
    
    A
    B
    C
👉 Parent constructor executes before child.

6. this vs super (🔥 Must-Know Table)
| Feature     | `this`                              | `super`       |
| ----------- | ----------------------------------- | ------------- |
| Refers to   | Current object                      | Parent object |
| Variables   | Current class                       | Parent class  |
| Methods     | Current class                       | Parent class  |
| Constructor | `this()`                            | `super()`     |
| Position    | First statement only in constructor | Same          |

7. Rules & Restrictions (Tricky)
  ❌ this() and super() cannot be together
  ❌ Cannot use super in static context
  ❌ Cannot access private parent members via super
  ✔ this can be returned

8. this and super with Static Context
  class Test {
      static void m1() {
          // this ❌
          // super ❌
      }
  }
  👉 Static methods belong to class, not object.

9. JVM / Memory Perspective
    this → reference to current heap object
    super → reference to parent part of same object
    Only one object is created (child)
    Parent variables are part of child object

10. this & super in Spring Boot (Important)
  Dependency Injection & this
  @Service
  class UserService {
  
      @Autowired
      private Repo repo;
  
      void process() {
          this.repo.save(); // optional
      }
  }
this rarely required
Used mainly for clarity or method chaining

12. Common Tricky Interview Questions (4+ Years)
Q1. Can this be null?
❌ No — it always refers to current object

Q2. Can we use super.super?
❌ No — only immediate parent

Q3. Is super() mandatory?
✔ Implicitly added if parent has default constructor

Q4. Can we override constructor?
❌ No — constructors are not inherited

Q5. Why super() must be first?
To ensure parent initialization before child

Q6. Difference between this.x and x?
No difference unless variable shadowing exists
